        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The numbered ring right behind the chuck is the clutch, and each number is a torque limit: the higher the number, the harder the drill twists before the clutch slips and makes that ratcheting click. The 1 and 2 on top of the drill are the gear selector, where 1 is low speed with high torque for driving screws and 2 is high speed for drilling holes, while the drill-bit icon on the clutch ring locks the clutch out and the hammer icon turns on hammer action for masonry. Everything else, the volts and amp-hours on the battery and the torque and RPM figures on the box, describes how much work the drill can do and for how long. None of it is complicated once it is decoded, and getting the clutch right is the single easiest way to stop stripping screw heads and sinking screws too deep.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Do All the Markings on a Drill Actually Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical cordless drill carries four groups of markings, and each one controls or describes something different. The clutch ring behind the chuck sets a torque limit. The gear switch on top picks a speed range. The icons on the clutch ring pick a mode. And the numbers on the battery and in the spec sheet tell you how strong the tool is and how long it will run. People tend to lump them together because they all look like numbers, but they answer four separate questions.
          </p>

          <p>
            The clutch ring usually runs from 1 up to somewhere between 15 and 24, depending on the model. Those numbers are not a measurement in inch-pounds or newton-meters. They are relative steps on that one drill, so a 10 on one brand is not the same force as a 10 on another. All the numbers promise is that each step lets the drill push a little harder than the one before it before the clutch gives way.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Marking</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where it is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it means</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 to 15-24</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ring behind the chuck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clutch setting: a torque limit, higher means more twist before slipping</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drill-bit icon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">End of the clutch ring</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clutch bypassed: full torque, used for drilling holes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hammer icon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clutch ring or mode collar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hammer action on, for masonry (hammer drills only)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 and 2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slider on top of the motor housing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gear: 1 is low speed and high torque, 2 is high speed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12V, 18V, 20V MAX</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Battery and tool label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Battery voltage class, a rough guide to power</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.5Ah to 8.0Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Battery label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Amp-hours: fuel tank size, longer runtime</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">in-lbs, RPM, BPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Box and spec sheet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Peak torque, spindle speed, hammer blows per minute</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There are a few smaller markings worth knowing too. The chuck often has its capacity stamped on it, such as 1/2 in or 13 mm, which is the largest bit shank it can grip. Many drills have a forward and reverse button above the trigger with small arrows, plus a center lock position that stops the trigger from firing when you set the drill down or carry it in a bag. Some newer models add an LED mode button or a speed-limiting dial, but the four main groups cover nearly every drill sold for home use.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only learn one marking, learn the clutch. It is the one that changes the result of every screw you drive, and it is the one most people leave wherever it happened to be when the drill came out of the box. The rest of this guide goes through each group in turn and finishes with a plain chart of which setting to use for common jobs. If you are shopping rather than decoding the drill you already own, our <a href="/best-cordless-drills/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best cordless drills</a> guide compares current models on the specs that matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clutch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Do the Numbers on the Clutch Ring Do?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The clutch is a torque-limiting mechanism between the motor and the chuck. Inside, a set of spring-loaded balls or teeth press against a plate. Turning the numbered ring changes how hard that spring presses. When the resistance at the bit exceeds the spring force, the plate slips, the chuck stops turning even though the motor keeps going, and you hear the familiar rapid clicking. At a low number the spring is light and the clutch slips early. At a high number it takes much more resistance before anything slips.
          </p>

          <p>
            The point of the clutch is control. When you drive a screw, the resistance climbs steadily as the threads bite and then jumps sharply when the head meets the surface. A well-chosen clutch setting lets the screw seat flush and then slips at that jump, so the screw stops where you want it instead of burying itself in drywall, splitting a thin board or snapping off. It also protects the screw head, because the bit stops turning before it can cam out and chew the recess.
          </p>

          <p>
            Low numbers suit small screws and soft materials: hinge screws, cabinet hardware, electrical cover plates, plastic parts and anything going into particleboard or drywall. Middle numbers suit ordinary wood screws in softwood, such as 1 1/4 in to 2 in screws in pine. High numbers suit longer and fatter screws, hardwood and lag screws into a pilot hole, where the drill needs more twist to finish the last turn. There is no universal chart because every drill and every screw is a little different, which is why the test-screw method later in this guide works better than memorizing numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            A common misunderstanding is that the clutch number sets speed. It does not. The trigger and the gear switch control speed, and the clutch only decides the point at which the drill gives up. You can drive slowly at a high clutch setting or quickly at a low one. Another misunderstanding is that a higher number makes the drill stronger. It cannot exceed the motor's maximum; the top clutch setting simply lets more of the motor's torque reach the bit before slipping.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Start low and work up.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Set the clutch a few numbers lower than you think you need, drive one test screw into scrap or an out-of-sight spot, and raise it one or two clicks at a time until the screw seats flush. Then leave it there for the rest of that batch.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One practical note: the clutch only works in the driving positions on the ring. Once you rotate past the highest number to the drill-bit icon, the clutch is locked out and the drill will keep twisting until something stalls or breaks. That is the right setting for boring holes and the wrong one for driving small screws, which is how many people strip their first few screws on a new drill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="symbols" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Do the Drill and Hammer Symbols Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the end of the numbered range you will usually find a small drill-bit icon. This is drill mode, and it bypasses the clutch completely so the full output of the motor reaches the chuck. Use it for drilling holes in wood, metal and plastic, and for heavy work such as spade bits, hole saws and auger bits, where a clutch slipping halfway through a cut would only slow you down. In drill mode, the only thing that stops the chuck is the motor stalling or you letting go of the trigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because nothing slips in drill mode, a bit that grabs, especially a large spade bit or hole saw breaking through the back of a board, can twist the drill hard against your wrist. Use the side handle if the drill came with one, keep a firm two-handed grip on big bits, and use low gear, which trades speed for control. Many current drills include electronic kickback protection that cuts power when the housing suddenly rotates, but it is a backup, not a reason to hold the drill loosely.
          </p>

          <p>
            A hammer icon appears only on hammer drills. Selecting it engages a mechanism that pounds the chuck forward and back thousands of times a minute while it spins, which chips away at brick, block and concrete far faster than rotation alone. It does nothing useful in wood or metal and can wander or tear the hole, so switch back to drill mode for those. On some drills the hammer icon sits on the same ring as the clutch numbers; on others it is a separate collar or slider.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your drill has the numbers and the drill icon but no hammer, it is a drill driver, and it can still drill small holes in brick with a masonry bit if you are patient. For anchors in concrete or a lot of masonry work, a hammer drill saves real time. Our <a href="/hammer-drill-vs-drill-driver/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hammer drill vs drill driver</a> comparison covers when the hammer function earns its extra weight and cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also see a screw icon at the start of the clutch range on some models, which simply marks the driving zone. Impact drivers are a different tool and use a different system: they have no clutch ring at all, and the 1, 2 and 3 or the mode button on an impact driver selects speed and impact power rather than a torque limit. The <a href="/drill-vs-impact-driver/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">drill vs impact driver</a> guide explains why that design makes them faster at long screws but less precise with small ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Do the 1 and 2 on Top of the Drill Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The slider on top of the motor housing marked 1 and 2 is a two-speed gearbox, not a power switch. Position 1 is low gear: the chuck turns more slowly, often somewhere in the range of 0 to 400 or 0 to 600 RPM on an 18V or 20V drill, and the gearing multiplies torque. Position 2 is high gear, commonly in the range of 0 to 1,500 or 0 to 2,000 RPM, with less torque available at the bit. The 0 at the start of each range is there because the trigger is variable: squeeze lightly for slow, all the way for the top speed of that gear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use gear 1 for driving screws, especially long ones, and for large-diameter bits like hole saws, spade bits and self-feed bits, where you want torque and control rather than speed. Use gear 2 for drilling small and medium holes with twist bits in wood, plastic and thin metal, where higher speed makes a cleaner hole faster. For metal thicker than sheet stock, drop back to low gear, because twist bits overheat and dull quickly when they spin too fast in steel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shift only when the motor is stopped. Moving the slider while the chuck is spinning, or while the drill is under load, grinds the gear teeth and can damage the gearbox over time. If the slider feels stuck halfway, bump the trigger briefly with the bit free so the gears line up, then try again. A few drills have three speeds instead of two, adding a faster third gear for small bits; the same rules apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some people assume 2 means more power because the number is bigger. In practice, high gear is the weaker gear for twisting effort. If the drill bogs down or the clutch keeps slipping on a big screw in gear 2, switching to gear 1 often solves it without changing anything else. Equally, if a small pilot hole is taking forever in gear 1, you are leaving speed on the table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Do the Volts and Amp-Hours on the Battery Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The voltage on the battery and tool, typically 12V, 18V or 20V MAX for drills, is the class of platform. More cells in series means more voltage, and more voltage generally lets the manufacturer build a stronger motor. Most 12V class packs contain three lithium-ion cells and most 18V and 20V MAX packs contain five. The difference between 18V and 20V MAX is a labeling convention rather than a power gap, which our <a href="/18v-vs-20v-drill/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">18V vs 20V drill</a> guide explains in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The amp-hour rating, written Ah, is the size of the fuel tank. A 2.0Ah pack holds roughly twice the charge of a 1.0Ah pack at the same voltage, so it runs about twice as long on the same job. Common drill batteries run from 1.5Ah compact packs up to 5.0Ah, 6.0Ah or 8.0Ah. Bigger packs weigh more and add bulk under the handle, so a lot of people prefer a 2.0Ah pack for overhead and cabinet work and save the big pack for drilling sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Multiplying volts by amp-hours gives watt-hours, the most honest single number for stored energy. An 18V 2.0Ah pack holds about 36 watt-hours, a 12V 2.0Ah pack about 24, and an 18V 5.0Ah pack about 90. That is why a 12V pack and an 18V pack with the same Ah rating do not run equally long on equal work: the 18V pack simply stores more energy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some packs also carry a small fuel gauge with three or four LEDs, which shows remaining charge when you press the button, and some show a symbol for a high-output or high-performance cell type. None of those markings change how you set the drill. They only tell you how long you can keep going before you need the charger, a topic covered in depth in our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-cordless-drill-batteries-last/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how long cordless drill batteries last</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Do Torque, RPM and BPM Ratings Mean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Torque on the box is usually given in inch-pounds, written in-lbs, or in newton-meters outside the US. It describes the maximum twisting force the drill can produce, measured at or near stall. Compact 12V drills often land somewhere around 250 to 400 in-lbs, typical 18V and 20V drill drivers somewhere around 400 to 700, and premium brushless hammer drills claim well over 1,000. Higher torque matters for big bits and long fasteners. For hanging shelves and assembling furniture, almost any modern drill has enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat torque claims with some caution when comparing brands. They are peak figures measured in the manufacturer's own way, and the drill rarely runs at that point in real use. Some brands do not list torque at all and use their own power metrics instead; DeWalt, for example, rates many tools in unit watts out, which combines speed and torque into one figure. Comparing two drills from the same brand is meaningful. Comparing a torque number from one brand to a different kind of number from another is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            RPM is spindle speed, and you will usually see two ranges for a two-speed drill, one per gear. Higher top speed helps small bits cut cleanly and quickly, but it does not make a drill better at driving screws. BPM, blows per minute, appears only on hammer drills and describes how fast the hammer mechanism pounds in hammer mode. Figures in the range of 20,000 to 30,000 BPM or more are common on 18V and 20V hammer drills, and more blows generally means faster progress in masonry.
          </p>

          <p>
            The final number worth reading is chuck size. A 1/2 in chuck accepts bit shanks up to half an inch and is standard on most 18V and 20V drills. A 3/8 in chuck is common on compact and 12V drills and limits you to smaller shanks, which rarely matters at home because most bits under half an inch have reduced shanks anyway. Brushless motors are another spec you will see; our <a href="/brushless-vs-brushed-drill/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">brushless vs brushed drill</a> guide explains why that label matters more for runtime than for raw torque.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-setting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Clutch Setting Should You Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because clutch numbers are relative, the most reliable method is a test screw. Set gear 1, pick a clutch number around a third of the way up the ring, and drive one screw of the size you are about to use into the same material. If the clutch clicks before the head is flush, turn up one or two numbers and try again. If the screw sinks below the surface or the material crushes, turn it down. It takes about a minute and saves an afternoon of filling over-driven holes.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gear</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Clutch starting point</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small screws in plastic, cover plates, hinges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest settings, roughly 1 to 4</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall screws, cabinet hardware, particleboard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low, roughly 3 to 7</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood screws in softwood, 1 1/4 to 2 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Middle of the ring</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Long screws, hardwood, lag screws with pilot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Upper settings</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Twist bits in wood, plastic, sheet metal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill icon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spade bits, hole saws, metal over 1/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill icon</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Masonry bits in brick, block, concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 or 2 per manual</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hammer icon (hammer drills)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Treat the numbers in that chart as a place to start, not a rule. A 20-position clutch on one drill and a 24-position clutch on another reach very different torque at the same number, and screw type changes things too. Self-tapping construction screws need far less effort than old-style wood screws with no pilot hole. The material also varies within a single job: a screw that seats perfectly in pine may stall in a knot.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the clutch slips at the top setting and the screw still is not home, do not reach for drill mode straight away. First check that you are in gear 1, then drill or enlarge the pilot hole, then consider whether the job really belongs to an impact driver. Long structural screws and lag screws are exactly where an <a href="/best-impact-driver/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">impact driver</a> beats a drill, and forcing them in drill mode is how wrists get twisted and bits snap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, set the clutch back to a low number when you put the drill away. It is a good habit for two reasons: the next person to pick it up, often you, will not accidentally over-drive the first screw, and a low starting point nudges you into running the quick test rather than guessing. Many experienced users keep the drill at a modest clutch setting for driving and leave hole drilling to a second tool so they never have to keep spinning the ring back and forth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving screws in drill mode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The drill-bit icon disables the clutch, so the drill keeps twisting after the screw seats. That is how heads strip, drywall paper tears and small screws snap. For driving, always use a numbered clutch setting and raise it gradually, one click at a time, until the screw finishes flush.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking the clutch number sets speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The clutch only decides when the drill gives up. Speed comes from the trigger and the 1 and 2 gear switch. If you want to drive slowly, feather the trigger in gear 1; turning the clutch down does not slow the chuck, it just makes it slip sooner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shifting gears while the motor runs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving the 1 and 2 slider while the chuck is turning grinds the gearbox teeth and wears them over time. Release the trigger, let the chuck stop, then shift. If the slider will not seat, bump the trigger briefly with nothing loaded and try again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using hammer mode on wood or metal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The hammer icon makes the chuck pound as it turns, which helps only in brick, block and concrete. In wood or steel it makes rough, wandering holes and wears bits for no gain. Switch back to the plain drill icon for anything that is not masonry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing torque numbers across brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Torque ratings are peak figures measured each manufacturer's own way, and some brands use different units entirely. A higher number on one box does not prove a stronger drill than another brand's. Compare within a brand, and judge across brands on motor type, gearing and real-world reviews.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What number should I set my drill clutch to for drywall screws?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start low, roughly 3 to 7 on most drills, in gear 1. Drive one test screw and adjust until the head sits just below the paper surface without tearing it. If you hang a lot of drywall, a dedicated drywall screw gun or a dimpler bit sets depth more consistently than any clutch number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a higher clutch number stronger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A higher number lets more of the motor's torque reach the bit before the clutch slips, so it can drive bigger screws. It does not increase the motor's maximum output. The drill-bit icon allows all of it with no slipping at all, which is why that mode is for drilling holes rather than driving small screws.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my drill keep clicking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clicking means the clutch is slipping because resistance has exceeded the setting. Turn the clutch up a number or two, make sure you are in gear 1, and check that a pilot hole is drilled for larger screws. If it clicks while drilling a hole, you are on a clutch number instead of the drill icon.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use speed 1 or 2 to drill holes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use 2 for small and medium twist bits in wood and plastic, where speed gives a cleaner, faster hole. Use 1 for large bits such as hole saws and spade bits, and for drilling steel, where slower speed controls heat and keeps the bit from grabbing. Shift only with the motor stopped.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does the hammer symbol on a drill mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It selects hammer mode, which pounds the chuck in and out while it spins to break up masonry. It only appears on hammer drills and only helps in brick, block, stone and concrete with a carbide masonry bit. Use plain drill mode for wood, metal and plastic, where hammering just roughs up the hole.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do clutch numbers mean the same thing on every drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The numbers are relative steps on that particular drill, not a calibrated unit like inch-pounds. Setting 10 on one model may be noticeably stronger or weaker than 10 on another, and the number of positions varies too. That is why the test-screw method works better than any universal chart.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a bigger Ah battery make a drill more powerful?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly it makes it run longer. Amp-hours measure stored charge, so a 5.0Ah pack runs roughly two and a half times as long as a 2.0Ah pack on the same work. Larger packs can sometimes hold higher output under heavy load, but they add weight, so match the pack to the job.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

